# Stone skipping, Kokkos twin (demonstrator/stone_skip.cpp) at 2e52b0f
# THE CROSS-CHECK RUN. GPU/src/skip.cu measured this case at D = 48 on a T4 in
# FP32 (results/skip/skip_d48_attack20.dat); this is D = 32 on four CPU threads
# in FP64, so it is a DIFFERENT RESOLUTION as well as a different code and
# precision. The comparison worth making is the attack-angle nutation, which is
# what the gyroscopic term governs:
#
#   t/t0     attack D=48 CUDA   attack D=32 Kokkos
#   1.25          11.50              12.62      <- minimum, same instant
#   1.50          13.18              14.02
#   1.75          15.04              15.40
#   2.00          16.41              16.35
#   2.25          17.07              16.73      <- recovery, same instant
#
# Both put the minimum at t/t0 = 1.25 and the recovery at 2.25, agreeing within
# 1.1 deg and converging to 0.3. Rebound instant 1.49 (D=48) against 1.36
# (D=32); peak rebound vy +0.0702 U against +0.0665 U. Before the gyroscopic
# term was added the angle collapsed monotonically in both, so this oscillation
# is a behaviour that did not exist in the code before 2026-09-03.
#
# Stone skipping   6-DOF disc, Kokkos   Threads   precision FP64
#   256 x 128 x 96   D 32   thickness 6.4 cells (1:5)   rho_b/rho_H 2.6
